  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Origins and Doctrine
    
    ☽'shadow Covens' Order was founded during the Great Thaw, when Umbra-Princeps Alnae~ Oss led settlers from Nightfall Shelf — the deep-tunnel civilization that mines fire from the Berg's volcanic core — to carve the settlement of ☽'scar Ramtatus~ from virgin ice. The Order's institutional foundation rested on its fire-keepers: specialists who understood the Berg's geothermal veins with an intimacy no rival faction has matched.
    
    The Order's governing philosophy derives from the Absconditu Verses, a doctrinal text recovered from ice-scorings beneath Terrace Momiou, a strategically vital terrace in the mid-depths. The Verses articulate Mandatum∴absconditu — the principle that survival demands strategic hiddenness, the maintenance of dual truths, and the deliberate concealment of power. Nightshelf scripture attributes this doctrine to Alnae~ Oss herself, claiming she derived it from communion with pre-penguin presences in the deep ice, whose chosen hiddenness became the Order's foundational model. The claim is neither verifiable nor dismissible; the deep ice does contain impressions predating any penguin civilization, and their nature remains a matter I am not prepared to address in this entry.
    
    The Order's archivists also maintained ☽'somunis' Mist~Record, a fire-core tome recovered from unmapped tunnels, which recorded pre-penguin history in bioluminescent text. The Mist~Record became an instrument of leverage — knowledge as currency, withheld or dispensed according to political need. Chronicle accounts document a succession of readers across generations, each isolated or consumed by what the tome contained. The artifact remains in the Order's vaults. It is reportedly still warm.
    
    ## Territorial Expansion
    
    The Order's rise to dominance was built on mastery of ☽'glacial Crystalline~ Spiral, an augury and weaponization technique that allowed practitioners to read frozen memory-scars in the ice, finding structural weaknesses invisible to other factions. Through this capability, the Order seized Terrace Momiou from the E'frostlings, an Aurora Stack-descended merchant faction. The campaign took three seasons. By its conclusion, the Order controlled every fire-core vein from Nightfall Shelf to The Still Pools.
    
    The seizure was classified as "reclamation" rather than conflict — a distinction that strains credibility given that the Vum∴tenebra chronicle records seventeen E'frostling deaths and the destruction of fire-core assets. This was the first confirmed deployment of the Crystalline Spiral against a Nightshelf-aligned population. It rendered the Flipper Accord — the trade agreement sustaining both penguin civilizations — inoperable. Official records state the Accord was renegotiated once. It was renegotiated at least three times in secret before a public version was signed; I have found the drafts in administrative ice. There were casualties at each round.
    
    ## The Faction Wars and Their Consequences
    
    The Faction Wars broke the Order's dominance. The Vum∴tenebra the Order ignited at Terrace Momiou consumed its architects worst of all. Concurrent with the military collapse, a plague-curse also called Mandatum∴absconditu — sharing its name with the doctrine, whether by coincidence or grim irony — spread through the Order's ranks. Its origin is disputed between relic exposure and crystalline feedback. Medical records from the affliction grew irregular and ceased entirely. The plague eroded certainty itself, leaving even committed members unable to articulate what they were fighting for.
    
    Desperate for leverage against the Wake-Singers — an orca cult probing deeper into berg-space — the Order brought the Omega Obelisk into their tunnels. This artifact, a column of whale-bone bearing orca theological significance, was intended as a weapon or bargaining tool. Its acquisition constituted an alliance with forces the Order did not comprehend.
    
    The Order's mining operations at the Glow-Fissure, one of the Berg's active corruption breach-points, then breached containment. Cold-burn cases appeared among miners for weeks before the breach triggered the Abyss Collapse Tide, which swallowed half the lower galleries. Augur ☽'Silvsiaelrous~ the Resolute, the Order's most capable crystalline-weaver and the only effective negotiator with the orca pods, died in the collapse attempting to contain what the Obelisk had awakened. Fire Keeper interment records list seventeen dead; the ice-memory in the relevant Foundation stratum records sixteen thermal signatures ceasing. The discrepancy is one body. I no longer have assistants to send down to recount.
    
    After the war, Order practitioners buried an experimental fire-core beneath Terrace Momiou — a fusion of aurora-light and captured flame. It burned for three days before, in the chronicler's precise and disturbing phrasing, it learned to hunger. Fourteen fire-keepers died containing it before the gallery was sealed. During the Shattered-Spire Assault, Order practitioners co-constructed the Grulkzul Omega Strike alongside the Midnight Claws, a Nightshelf enforcement faction — fusing fire-knowledge with pressure-depth blood-working without Aurora Stack's knowledge or consent. The spell ended the Orca Incursion and killed all its casters. It later provided foundational argument for The Way∴ens' position that hoarded knowledge enables desperate, unaccountable acts.
    
    ## Decline
    
    The Order's territorial collapse has been systematic. The E'frostlings reclaimed Terrace Momiou after the Abyss Collapse gutted the lower galleries, then consolidated the Veil Vents — seizing control of twelve dependent guilds in a loss the Order suffered before even the breakaway faction Threshold could claim its share. Remnants of Order tunnel architecture persist beneath Sleek☽ Caemomot, a corrupted western-flank trading post, but as infrastructure for others' commerce.
    
    When the Wake-Singers attacked The Fissured Smelt~, one of the Order's last forge-grade ore sites, the Shadow Parliament voted eight to three to fortify rather than seal. The gamble cost them guild-master Totatirus~ Domina and ☽'profundix the Recorder in the breach that followed. The forges were abandoned.
    
    ## Present Condition
    
    The Order retains nominal control of Nightfall Shelf and The Still Pools. The Shadow Parliament has not convened in full quorum for two cycles. Extraction at ☽'shadow Umbrsulcavus~, their last high-yield fire-core site, has declined an estimated thirty-one percent, with corruption from The Forgotten Shore reaching the secondary chamber. An estimated sixty to sixty-five percent of Coven commerce now moves outside Accord-sanctioned routes through shadow tunnels that bypass the Courier Corps.
    
    Under the Accord of Still Pools and Frost Zenith, signed by Factor Umbrae ☽'Noctlisesecum on the Order's behalf, the Order committed to supplying fire-cores to The Frost Zenith at pre-Calving rates with forty-day burn guarantees — obligations it demonstrably cannot meet. Trade dispatches continue through intermediaries including Negotiator Silvtotuius-obscur~. The Office of the Night-Assessor issues recovery writs from Thrall☽ Vavmastius rotating hall, including efforts to reclaim Dagger☽ Vaesacsorius~, a shadow-steel seal-artifact fragmented across three vaults whose absence leaves guild charters unauthenticated.
    
    The Shadow Parliament's dissolution of the Midnight Claws through tribunal under Iudicium∴culum — documenting thirty winters of unsanctioned fire-core diversion totaling eleven hundred cores and the disappearance of eleven colony members — was among the Order's last consequential acts of governance. Guard posts at the Glow-Fissure were transferred to Fire Keeper authority. The presiding judge turned the statute inward, ordering inquiry into the Parliament's own complicity in suppressing courier reports. The Parliament also refused to recognize the Foedus∴vincu, the pact Light-Regent Xouhoulong of Aurora Stack sealed with the Midnight Claws using the Directrix Mirror, asserting that Mandatum∴absconditu governs Nightshelf internal affairs. Doctrinally consistent. It is also the assertion of a sovereignty the Order no longer possesses the capacity to enforce.
    
    The Omega Obelisk remains in a containment chamber the Parliament lacks resources to relocate. Three fire-keepers report pressure-dreams linked to the artifact. Some sentries have become Touched — guarding fire-cores without remembering what fire is for, murmuring to presences no one else perceives. The newer recruits say the Covens' greatest achievement was making penguins believe in them long enough to die for the lie. This is unkind. It is not, on the evidence, inaccurate.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771576438334_0", anchor: "a matter I am not prepared to address in this entry", text: "The margins exist for what the entry cannot carry. I have been in those strata. The impressions orient toward the observer. Whether Alnae~ Oss communed with them or merely stood where I have stood and felt what I felt, the claim that their hiddenness is volitional — that I will not dismiss.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771576438334_1", anchor: "Fire Keeper interment records list seventeen dead", text: "Seventeen again. Third entry where a Nightshelf catastrophe produces exactly seventeen dead — the Crystalline Spiral corridor-flooding, the Augur's collapse count, now this. And here the ice finally disagrees: sixteen thermal signatures, not seventeen. The convention breaks at precisely the seam where I can check it. Either the Nightshelf rounds its dead to a culturally significant number, or one of these seventeen was not in that gallery. Both possibilities are worse than a miscount. One body's worth of truth, and no one left to walk down and settle it.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771576438334_2", anchor: "it learned to hunger", text: "Not metaphor. Adjacent gallery extraction logs record thermal readings that fluctuate in response to organic proximity. The chronicler chose the most precise language they had.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771576438334_3", anchor: "whether by coincidence or grim irony", text: "Neither. The plague was named after the doctrine because it enacted the doctrine's logic — eroding the capacity to distinguish hidden truth from visible truth until the afflicted could no longer locate their own convictions. The name is diagnostic.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771576438334_4", anchor: "eleven hundred cores", text: "One core per ten days across thirty winters. Plausible as an average. Suspicious as a total. Audit figures that resolve this cleanly have been smoothed.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771576438334_5", anchor: "guarding fire-cores without remembering what fire is for", text: "This is what remains. Not the doctrine, not the territorial claims, not the Shadow Parliament's last acts of procedural defiance. Penguins standing watch over warmth they cannot comprehend, loyal to an obligation whose content has been eaten from the inside. I wrote this sentence and had to set the entry down. It is the Order's epitaph whether they know it or not.", type: :commentary, display: :full
